Evidence per H2 (do not attribute beyond source):

H2-1: Williams (years, no "overnight fungus"); early stage often painless; AAD: first sign is a color change on part of a nail.
H2-2: AAD: dirty nails alone cannot cause it; fungi must enter the nail or a microscopic skin opening. Feet live in shoes all day. Washing without drying between toes can raise risk.
H2-3: Williams (self-infection); AAD: athlete's foot to nail is a common route, "you may not even know you have athlete's foot" (Dr. Elewski). Shared clippers, towels, wet communal floors still spread it.
H2-4: Williams (sister's cure claim, "no way"); AAD: prescription medication is needed, a 3-month course is typical; WebMD: nothing kills toenail fungus right away (paraphrase).
H2-5: Dr. Hull (Utah Health): vinegar and tea tree oil don't penetrate the nail well; Vicks probably won't help but won't hurt.
H2-6: AAD: almost never goes away on its own, can spread, can cause pain and misshapen nails; it is treatable, and early is easier. Diabetes raises ulcer risk (Berg).
H2-7: Risk rises with age, but athletes, gym-goers, and tight-shoe wearers get it at any age.
H2-8: Dr. Hull confirms with clippings/cultures because other conditions mimic fungus; injury and psoriasis can look identical. Do not link out to post 13 (not in locked list).
H2-9: AAD: treatment can clear the infection but discoloration and lifting stay until the nail grows out; big toe may take a year or longer.
